think Read the selection ladder to see how a selection is represented: hit_test, region_select, lasso_select all return an ASCENDING SET of dense labels []u32. scene_manifest carries per-object rows (area, box, Euler, holes). The crux-first next rung is what a surface DOES with a selection: JOIN scene_manifest facts over just the selected subset. It composes over ANY selector since all speak the same label-set language -- the natural cap of the selection family, closing cleanly with a GREEN witness, no fetch/key/funds gate.
think The decisive crux: a selection is a SUBSET, so a read over it is strictly sharper than the same read over the whole scene -- the scene's largest object AND its largest_holed can both lie outside the selection. Chose fixtures that make it concrete: {small ring, small block} excludes both the scene largest (block 25) and largest_holed (large ring 16), so largest_in_selection and largest_holed_in_selection both name the small ring; the filter counts one holed of two; {small ring, large ring} sorts by area to reverse the ascending label order 2,3 -> 3,2.
think Confirmed no name collision: part_query is the marketplace text-search box, region_stats operates over []Region; selection_summary is free and composes over scene_manifest + []u32.
obs Built image/selection_summary.rye (total_selected_area, largest_in_selection, holed_in_selection_count, largest_holed_in_selection, sort_selection_by_area over a validated selection; validate_selection refuses BadSelection at the edge) + tools/selection_summary_witness.rish (native contains, REDS #85).
obs Two reds caught before GREEN, both by the build/lint on metal, neither an algorithm red so neither booked: (1) the print's `{small ring, small block}` braces read as Zig format placeholders -- the same brace-escape trap region_select hit -- fixed by doubling to `{{...}}`; (2) TAME flagged two compound `assert(a and b)` lines, split so the failing half is named. Witness GREEN on the next run, tame_style_check GREEN, width-check exit 0, both files plain 7-bit ASCII.
obs Sort is a bounded insertion sort by (area descending, label ascending); since a selection arrives ascending by label, equal-area ties keep ascending label order. Postcondition asserts the result is non-increasing by area over the whole slice.
